{
 "_doc": "Crew leveling (change crew-leveling, mockup 39; owner, 2026-11-12: \"ensigns provide no bonus but gain xp overtime, and can be traded for prestige so you can get more experienced crew, or just level up your crew member\", \"need crew skill tree\", \"skill tree, each level gets a skill point, the player can auto level officers or pick paths on the tree to go down and specialize\", \"I wish crew leveling was kind of like a pyramid tree or something where some nodes can cross over, and some nodes have 2 or more prereqs\", \"it doesnt have to be like an actual pyramid... just a skill tree\"). After the Starfleet Command 3 manual's officers (printed page 36: officers gain experience over a campaign, their worth and skills rising, and are swapped for more experienced ones from a pool for prestige; three skills to a station), in our own words and numbers. levels: the highest level; an officer has one skill point for each level above the first, so a level 1 officer (an ensign) has none and brings nothing. ranks and rank_level: the rank names and the level each begins at. xp_next: experience to reach each next level from level 1, spent on the promotion. xp_battle: experience to every officer of the crew for a battle fought, times grade_x by the mission's grade. xp_turn: experience to every officer for each campaign turn served. xp_per_prestige: the rate between experience and prestige, both ways: an officer transferred out is worth worth_per_level for each level above the first plus their banked experience at this rate, and a promotion's shortfall may be paid in prestige at it. hire_per_level: what an officer costs to hire from the pool, in prestige, for each level above the first. pool_levels: the levels on offer at each station each turn: five ensigns and three experienced officers at different levels (owner, 2026-11-06: \"Always 3 experienced of various levels and 5 cadets\"). retrain: the prestige to take back every point an officer has placed. trees: for each station a skill tree of fourteen skills, each taken with one point: themes names its three lanes (left, centre, right); every skill has a lane (0 to 2, a crossover halfway between two) and a depth (1, a root, to 5, the capstone), which each layout of the page places its own way; all: open only once every one of these is learned; any: open once one of these is learned. role: root, step, crossover (open from either of the two lanes beside it), keystone (needs both crossovers), tip (needs its own lane and the crossover beside it) or capstone (above the keystone). fx: the abilities it changes, each the key its battle rule reads and the change per skill; icon: the picture it shows (docs/mockups/lib/skill-icons.js), its first ability; text says the same in words. auto: the order an officer on auto level spends points in, up the centre to the capstone and then the left lane; past it, the first open skill. layout: where the tree's skills stand on its stage (the mockup's Branches and the game's crew tab, scaled to their space): stage_px the stage's width and height, lane_x the first lane's x and the step to the next (a crossover halfway), tier_y each depth's y from the top, themes_y the lane names' y (owner, 2026-11-13: \"taller where the nodes branch out please, lets do branches\").",
